  "refuter_findings": [
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:1",
      "id": "fs-every-profinite-group-is-pro-p-for-some-prime",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "It asserts “the quotient onto Z_q/pZ_q ≅ Z/qZ is a nontrivial q-group” for q ≠ p. But p is a unit in Z_q when q ≠ p, so pZ_q=Z_q and Z_q/pZ_q=0. The required nontrivial quotient is Z_q/qZ_q, not the displayed quotient.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "111591afa66bbfbd490b65206bd16e3507374ef7ef1daa0f5091d0acd0b298f3"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:2",
      "id": "thm-frattini-formula-without-an-extra-closure-for-finitely-generated-pro-p-groups",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Statement calls Φ(G)=[G,G]G^p false for every finitely generated pro-p group. Its cited dependency establishes only “Φ(G)=overline([G,G]G^p)”, so its step 2.1 does not license an assertion that this raw subgroup is dense proper. In the stated finitely generated domain, [G,G]G^p is open and therefore closed, hence equals its closure and Φ(G); see [Wilkes, Profinite Groups and Group Cohomology](https://www.dpmms.cam.ac.uk/~grw46/LectureNotes.pdf).",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "42763b698b699b1f86f4e2d0f2b3be35b4360e9679dca48faeb2c0142ed35a29"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:3",
      "id": "lem-maximal-proper-closed-subgroups-of-a-profinite-group-are-open",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 gives xN∩H=∅, hence x∉HN. It does not show “N⊈H”, as step 2.1 claims. Thus HN is a proper closed overgroup of H, so maximality gives HN=H and hence N≤H. The written argument instead says HN properly contains H, rules out HN=G, and then concludes HN=H; that conclusion does not follow from its asserted alternatives.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "97334cf3a9ae37e5e6482a22dc6208ea33f595d0aeb3a83e2829a17cbc4c29f2"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:4",
      "id": "thm-green-vertex-source-existence-and-conjugacy",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The Statement gives no condition char(k)=p. Take p=3, G=C_2, k=F_2, and M=k with trivial action. The only 3-subgroup is 1. If M were relatively 1-projective, it would be a summand of an induced module from 1, hence projective; but the trivial F_2C_2-module is not projective. Thus M has no 3-vertex, contradicting the unqualified Statement.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "58203c4601998eef95038e5ca214dbabcd4f69079fbdbf123391215bbbfe6f49"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:5",
      "id": "thm-green-vertex-source-existence-and-conjugacy",
      "location": "1.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The proof says “choose a minimal p-subgroup Q≤G for which M is relatively Q-projective” without establishing that this set is nonempty. Relative G-projectivity is automatic, but G need not be a p-subgroup. The cited Higman criterion only says relative H-projectivity is equivalent to an identity relative trace for some H-endomorphism; it supplies no such trace for a p-subgroup. A Sylow-relative-projectivity argument is required and is absent.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "58203c4601998eef95038e5ca214dbabcd4f69079fbdbf123391215bbbfe6f49"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:6",
      "id": "thm-inverse-limit-topology-equals-the-p-adic-metric-topology",
      "location": "1.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 says that agreement in the first n coordinates is equivalent to d_p(x,y)<p^{-n}. If x and y agree through coordinate n but differ at n+1, then ν_p(x,y)=n and d_p(x,y)=p^{-n}. Such y lies in U_n(x), but does not satisfy the strict inequality. The displayed set is described correctly using d_p≤p^{-n}, or with a shifted strict-radius index.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "3a463c18c334c39aa8fa3a97dfce80383378981e6f93a84ae0291216c359e863"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:7",
      "id": "thm-kg-is-local-iff-g-is-a-p-group",
      "location": "1.2",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "After finding a nonzero vector t in a nontrivial C_q-submodule of Res(k[G]), the proof says “choose a maximal left ideal M<k[G] that does not contain t.” The cited transversal result only identifies an induced module with a direct sum of copies of its source; it gives no reason that t avoids the Jacobson radical. A nonzero element can lie in every maximal left ideal, so this maximal ideal cannot be chosen from the preceding facts.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "3e356c29638d03dd96ed80fc0e5b5b5820c15da820eea0a4863163f2820f9bc0"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:8",
      "id": "thm-projective-covers-exist-and-are-unique-for-finite-dimensional-algebras",
      "location": "3.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "From bijectivity of gf and fg, the proof concludes “Therefore f and g are inverse isomorphisms.” This is false. Let A=k[ε]/(ε²), M=A/(ε), and let π=ρ:A→M be the quotient projective cover. Set f to multiplication by 1+ε and g=id_A. Then ρf=π and πg=ρ, while gf=fg is multiplication by 1+ε, not id_A. The proof has shown that f is an isomorphism, which suffices, but its asserted inverse relation is false.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "c3f1d10ab34af454d8eb6750e6c7f968787b9c6de85160e7184993880363b154"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:9",
      "id": "thm-zp-is-compact-hausdorff-totally-disconnected-and-complete",
      "location": "Facts & Assumptions [L1]",
      "defect": "citation-inaccurate",
      "evidence": "This fact says an inverse limit of finite discrete groups “is Hausdorff, compact, and totally disconnected.” The cited dependency’s Statement is: “An inverse limit of finite discrete groups is Hausdorff and totally disconnected. Assuming the Axiom of Choice, it is also compact.” Neither the Given nor the Statement here assumes the Axiom of Choice, so the citation has dropped a required hypothesis for compactness.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "31d7414f52f184055b0ddcdc5bdf5991b25d288c525578ad4646f43f7f7c9f7c"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:10",
      "id": "thm-zp-is-compact-hausdorff-totally-disconnected-and-complete",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "It says agreement in the first n coordinates “makes d_p(x^(m),a)<p^{-n}.” Exact agreement through n followed by disagreement at n+1 gives ν_p=n and d_p=p^{-n}, not a strict inequality. This repeats the off-by-one error in the cited metric-topology theorem.",
      "severity": "fatal",
